CertHub.register({"id":"aws-cloud-practitioner","vendor":"Amazon Web Services","name":"AWS Certified Cloud Practitioner","short":"Cloud Practitioner","exam":"CLF-C02","blurb":"Entry-level AWS cloud fundamentals for technical and non-technical roles: cloud value and economics, the shared responsibility model and core security services, the main AWS compute, storage, database and networking services, and how AWS pricing, billing and support work.","status":"check","statusNote":"Domains and weights (Cloud Concepts 24%, Security and Compliance 30%, Cloud Technology and Services 34%, Billing, Pricing, and Support 12%) come from the published CLF-C02 exam guide as best known. The AWS certification pages and exam guide could not be opened from this environment during this check on Sept 25, 2026, so confirm the domains, weights, exam length (65 questions, 90 minutes) and passing score (700) on the official exam page before booking, and check whether AWS has announced a successor exam. AWS has also been revising its Support plan lineup, so confirm current plan names and features on the AWS Support plans page.","lastVerified":"2026-09-25","notices":[],"examInfo":{"questions":"65 (50 scored, 15 unscored; multiple choice and multiple response)","minutes":90,"pass":"700 on a 100–1000 scale","extra":"No prerequisites. AWS recommends up to about 6 months of exposure to the AWS Cloud in any role (technical, managerial, sales, purchasing or financial). The certification is valid for 3 years."},"examSim":{"questions":65,"minutes":90},"sources":[{"label":"AWS Certified Cloud Practitioner exam page","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/certification/certified-cloud-practitioner/"},{"label":"CLF-C02 exam guide (AWS documentation)","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-certification/latest/cloud-practitioner-02/cloud-practitioner-02.html"},{"label":"CLF-C02 exam guide (PDF)","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/pdfs/aws-certification/latest/cloud-practitioner-02/cloud-practitioner-02.pdf"}],"planWeeks":6,"hoursPerWeek":"5–7","domains":[{"id":1,"name":"Cloud Concepts","w":24,"topics":["Benefits of the AWS Cloud: pay-as-you-go pricing, economies of scale, agility, elasticity and global reach","The six advantages of cloud computing, including trading fixed expense for variable expense and no longer guessing capacity","High availability, fault tolerance, scalability and elasticity: what each term means and how AWS delivers it","AWS Well-Architected Framework: the six pillars and what each one is responsible for","Cloud design principles: loose coupling, designing for failure, automation and operations as code","The AWS Well-Architected Tool and running a Well-Architected review","AWS Cloud Adoption Framework (AWS CAF): the six perspectives and the business benefits of adoption","Migration strategies (the 7 Rs): rehost, replatform, refactor, repurchase, retire, retain and relocate","Migration tools: AWS Application Migration Service, AWS DMS with the Schema Conversion Tool, and AWS DataSync","Cloud economics: fixed vs variable costs, total cost of ownership and the costs that move to AWS","Licensing strategies (bring your own license vs license included) and rightsizing to cut waste"],"notes":["Exam guide tasks 1.1–1.4: benefits of the AWS Cloud, design principles, migration benefits and strategies, cloud economics"],"labs":["No account needed: read the free AWS Well-Architected Framework overview and, for a simple two-tier web app (web servers plus a database), write one sentence per pillar describing how the app would meet it. Then list which cloud design principles (loose coupling, automation, design for failure) your design uses.","Free AWS account: open the AWS Well-Architected Tool (no charge for the tool itself), define a sample workload and answer the Reliability and Cost Optimization pillar questions, then read the improvement plan it produces. Do not create any real resources for this lab.","No account needed: use the AWS Pricing Calculator to estimate a small web server (one small burstable EC2 instance, 30 GB of EBS and 50 GB of S3) for one year, then list the on-premises costs the estimate replaces (hardware, power, cooling, space, hardware refresh, admin time) to see fixed vs variable expense in practice."]},{"id":2,"name":"Security and Compliance","w":30,"topics":["The AWS shared responsibility model: security of the cloud vs security in the cloud","How responsibilities shift across Amazon EC2, Amazon RDS, AWS Lambda and Amazon S3","Compliance and governance: AWS Artifact, AWS Audit Manager, AWS Config and where to find compliance information","Encryption at rest and in transit: AWS KMS, AWS CloudHSM and AWS Certificate Manager","Protecting the root user: MFA, no access keys, and the tasks that require root credentials","IAM users, groups, roles and policies, and the principle of least privilege","Workforce single sign-on with AWS IAM Identity Center and customer sign-in with Amazon Cognito","Storing credentials safely: AWS Secrets Manager and AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store","Network protection: security groups vs network ACLs, AWS WAF, AWS Shield Standard and Advanced, AWS Firewall Manager","Threat detection and posture: Amazon GuardDuty, Amazon Inspector, Amazon Macie, Amazon Detective and AWS Security Hub","Logging and monitoring for security: AWS CloudTrail, Amazon CloudWatch and AWS Trusted Advisor security checks","Where to get security help: AWS security documentation, AWS Knowledge Center, AWS Marketplace security products and AWS Partners"],"notes":["Exam guide tasks 2.1–2.4: shared responsibility model, security, governance and compliance concepts, access management, security components and resources"],"labs":["Free AWS account: turn on MFA for the root user, create an administrator identity (IAM Identity Center user, or an IAM user in an admin group) and stop using root. Then create a group with the AWS managed ReadOnlyAccess policy, sign in as a member and confirm you cannot launch an EC2 instance. IAM and IAM Identity Center have no charge.","Free AWS account: in AWS Artifact, browse the available reports (for example a SOC or ISO document) and read what each proves. Then open CloudTrail Event history (free for 90 days of management events) and find the IAM changes you made in the previous lab: who made them, when and from which IP.","Free AWS account: open Trusted Advisor and review the free security checks (root MFA, S3 bucket permissions, security groups with unrestricted ports). Optional: enable GuardDuty's 30-day free trial, generate sample findings from its settings page and read them, then suspend or disable GuardDuty before the trial ends so you are not billed."]},{"id":3,"name":"Cloud Technology and Services","w":34,"topics":["Ways to use AWS: AWS Management Console, AWS CLI, SDKs, APIs and infrastructure as code with AWS CloudFormation","Deployment models (cloud, hybrid and on-premises) and one-time vs repeatable provisioning","AWS global infrastructure: Regions, Availability Zones, edge locations, Local Zones, Wavelength Zones and AWS Outposts","Choosing a Region: compliance and data residency, latency to users, service availability and price","Amazon EC2 instance families, AMIs, Elastic Load Balancing and Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling","Containers and serverless compute: Amazon ECS, Amazon EKS, AWS Fargate, AWS Lambda and AWS Elastic Beanstalk","Databases: Amazon RDS and Aurora, Amazon DynamoDB, Amazon ElastiCache, Amazon Redshift and other purpose-built databases","Networking: Amazon VPC, subnets, internet and NAT gateways, Route 53, CloudFront, Site-to-Site VPN and Direct Connect","Storage: Amazon S3 and its storage classes, Amazon EBS, instance store, Amazon EFS, Amazon FSx, AWS Storage Gateway and AWS Backup","AI and machine learning services: Amazon SageMaker AI, Amazon Bedrock, Amazon Q, and task-specific AI services such as Rekognition, Textract, Comprehend, Transcribe, Polly and Lex","Analytics services: Amazon Athena, AWS Glue, Amazon Kinesis, Amazon EMR and Amazon OpenSearch Service","Application integration, monitoring and other services: Amazon SQS, Amazon SNS, Amazon EventBridge, Amazon CloudWatch, AWS Systems Manager and AWS IoT Core"],"notes":["Exam guide tasks 3.1–3.8: deploying and operating in the cloud, global infrastructure, compute, database, network, storage, AI/ML and analytics, and other in-scope service categories"],"labs":["Free Tier or new-account credits: launch one Free Tier eligible EC2 instance in a public subnet with a security group that allows HTTP from anywhere and SSH only from your IP, install a web server with a user data script and browse to it. Then terminate the instance and check that no EBS volumes, Elastic IPs or snapshots are left behind.","Free Tier or new-account credits: create an S3 bucket (leave Block Public Access on), upload a few files, enable versioning, delete a file and restore the older version, and add a lifecycle rule that moves objects to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval after 30 days. Empty and delete the bucket when finished.","Free Tier or new-account credits: create a Lambda function in the console that runs when a file is uploaded to an S3 bucket and writes the object name to a DynamoDB table in on-demand mode; check the output in CloudWatch Logs. Delete the function, table, bucket and log group afterward, and check the Billing console the next day to confirm nothing is still running."]},{"id":4,"name":"Billing, Pricing, and Support","w":12,"topics":["EC2 purchase options: On-Demand, Reserved Instances, Savings Plans, Spot Instances, Dedicated Hosts and Dedicated Instances","Data transfer and storage pricing: inbound vs outbound traffic, cross-Region traffic and S3 storage class costs","AWS Free Tier offers and how to avoid unexpected charges","Estimating and tracking cost: AWS Pricing Calculator, AWS Cost Explorer and AWS Budgets","Detailed billing data: the Billing and Cost Management console, Cost and Usage Reports and data exports, and cost allocation tags","AWS Organizations: consolidated billing, combined volume discounts and shared Reserved Instance and Savings Plans discounts","Cost optimization tools: AWS Trusted Advisor, AWS Compute Optimizer and rightsizing recommendations","AWS Support plans and what each includes, including Technical Account Managers and AWS Health","Help and partner resources: AWS re:Post, Knowledge Center, AWS Marketplace, AWS Partner Network, AWS Professional Services and the AWS Trust & Safety team"],"notes":["Exam guide tasks 4.1–4.3: pricing models, billing, budget and cost management resources, technical resources and AWS Support options"],"labs":["Free AWS account: in Billing and Cost Management, create a zero-spend budget and a monthly cost budget of $5 with email alerts, and turn on Free Tier usage alerts. Do this before any other hands-on lab so a forgotten resource cannot run up a bill.","Free AWS account: open Cost Explorer (the console view is free) and group last month's cost by service and by Region. Then activate a user-defined cost allocation tag such as Project, tag the resources you create in the other labs, and after a day or two filter Cost Explorer by that tag.","No account needed: build a comparison table of the AWS Support plans from the official Support plans page (response times, Technical Account Manager, Trusted Advisor checks, price basis). Then look up one billing question on AWS re:Post and the AWS Knowledge Center and note which gave a clearer answer."]}],"study":{"1":[["What are the six advantages of cloud computing that AWS describes?","Trade fixed (capital) expense for variable expense; benefit from massive economies of scale; stop guessing capacity; increase speed and agility; stop spending money running and maintaining data centers; go global in minutes."],["What is the difference between scalability and elasticity?","Scalability is the ability to handle more load by adding resources (up or out). Elasticity is doing that automatically in both directions, adding resources when demand rises and removing them when it falls, so you pay only for what the current load needs."],["Name the six Well-Architected pillars.","Operational excellence, security, reliability, performance efficiency, cost optimization and sustainability."],["What are the six AWS CAF perspectives, and which are business-focused vs technical?","Business, people and governance are the business-focused perspectives; platform, security and operations are the technical ones."],["Compare rehost, replatform and refactor.","Rehost (lift and shift) moves an application unchanged, for example to EC2. Replatform makes a few cloud optimizations without changing the core architecture, such as moving a self-managed database to Amazon RDS. Refactor re-architects the application to use cloud-native features such as serverless or microservices."]],"2":[["State the shared responsibility model in one sentence each for AWS and the customer.","AWS is responsible for security of the cloud: the hardware, software, networking and facilities that run AWS services. The customer is responsible for security in the cloud: their data, identities and access, and configuration such as guest OS patches, firewalls and encryption settings."],["What are shared controls? Give examples.","Controls that apply to both layers, each party handling its own part: patch management (AWS patches infrastructure, customers patch their guest OS and apps), configuration management, and awareness and training."],["Why use IAM roles instead of access keys for applications on AWS?","A role hands out temporary credentials that rotate automatically, so there are no long-term keys to store in code or config files where they can leak."],["Compare security groups and network ACLs.","Security groups work at the instance (network interface) level, are stateful and have allow rules only. Network ACLs work at the subnet level, are stateless (return traffic must be allowed explicitly), support allow and deny rules and are evaluated in rule-number order."],["Which service does what: GuardDuty, Inspector, Macie, Detective, Security Hub?","GuardDuty detects threats from logs such as CloudTrail, VPC Flow Logs and DNS. Inspector scans EC2, container images and Lambda for software vulnerabilities. Macie finds sensitive data such as PII in S3. Detective helps investigate the root cause of findings. Security Hub gathers findings in one place and checks accounts against best-practice standards."],["What is the difference between CloudTrail, CloudWatch and AWS Config?","CloudTrail records API activity (who did what, when). CloudWatch collects metrics, logs and alarms about performance and operations. AWS Config records resource configurations over time and evaluates them against rules."]],"3":[["What is the relationship between Regions, Availability Zones and edge locations?","A Region is a geographic area containing multiple isolated Availability Zones, each made of one or more data centers with separate power and networking. Edge locations are separate sites, far more numerous, used by CloudFront, Route 53 and other services to serve users with low latency."],["When would you pick EBS, EFS, S3 or instance store?","EBS: persistent block storage attached to one EC2 instance, in one AZ. EFS: a shared NFS file system many Linux instances can mount across AZs. S3: durable object storage reached over HTTPS for any amount of data. Instance store: temporary block storage physically attached to the host, lost when the instance stops or terminates."],["Compare Amazon RDS, DynamoDB and Redshift.","RDS is a managed relational database (MySQL, PostgreSQL, MariaDB, Oracle, SQL Server, plus Aurora) for transactional workloads. DynamoDB is a serverless key-value and document database with single-digit millisecond performance at any scale. Redshift is a data warehouse for analytic SQL over very large datasets."],["What is the difference between Site-to-Site VPN and Direct Connect?","Site-to-Site VPN is an encrypted tunnel over the public internet, quick to set up but with variable performance. Direct Connect is a dedicated private connection from your premises to AWS with more consistent bandwidth and latency, but it takes longer to provision."],["When would you use Amazon Bedrock rather than Amazon SageMaker AI?","Bedrock gives API access to foundation models from Amazon and other providers for generative AI apps without managing infrastructure. SageMaker AI is for building, training and deploying your own machine learning models."],["Compare Amazon SQS and Amazon SNS.","SQS is a queue: messages wait until a consumer pulls and processes them, which decouples and buffers components. SNS is publish/subscribe: a message sent to a topic is pushed immediately to every subscriber (email, SMS, Lambda, SQS and others)."]],"4":[["When should you use On-Demand, Savings Plans or Reserved Instances, and Spot?","On-Demand for short-term, unpredictable or new workloads with no commitment. Savings Plans or Reserved Instances for steady usage you can commit to for 1 or 3 years, at a large discount. Spot for fault-tolerant, flexible work that can handle interruption, at the deepest discount."],["Which tool do you use to estimate, to analyze and to alert on cost?","AWS Pricing Calculator estimates the cost of an architecture before you build it. Cost Explorer shows and forecasts past and current spend. AWS Budgets alerts (and can run actions) when actual or forecast cost or usage crosses a threshold."],["What does consolidated billing in AWS Organizations give you?","One bill for all member accounts, combined usage for volume pricing tiers, and sharing of Reserved Instance and Savings Plans discounts across accounts, at no extra charge."],["What are cost allocation tags?","Key-value tags (AWS-generated or user-defined) that, once activated in the Billing console, appear in cost reports so you can break spend down by project, team or environment."],["Which Support plan includes a designated Technical Account Manager, and what do all plans include?","Enterprise Support includes a designated TAM. Every account, including Basic Support, gets customer service for account and billing questions, documentation and re:Post, AWS Health, and the core Trusted Advisor checks."]]},"qCount":150,"hasLessons":true,"hasLessonsEs":true,"hasPbqs":true,"hasHandson":true,"hasQuestionsEs":true,"hasLevels":true});
